This multi-resolution strategy offers several advantages over traditional single-resolution approaches. First, it enables training on widely available coarse-resolution reference GFSC data while still producing high-resolution outputs that exploit the full spatial capacity of modern satellite sensors. Second, the learned aggregation functions preserve critical spatial information during downsampling, avoiding information loss inherent in fixed aggregation methods like simple averaging. Third, the approach is generalizable to other earth observation applications facing similar resolution mismatches between high-resolution input sensors and coarse-resolution reference data. The trade-offs of this approach merit consideration. Training on coarse-resolution targets may limit the model's ability to capture sub-60m snow cover features that lack representation in the training signal. However, the incorporation of high-resolution auxiliary features (NDSI, backscatter textures) and the guided disaggregation framework partially mitigate this limitation by providing fine-scale structural information to guide the upsampling process. Future work could explore hybrid training strategies that combine coarse-resolution reference data with sparse high-resolution annotations to enhance sub-pixel prediction capabilities further.


\subsection{Cross-Modal Fusion Performance}

The architecture's multi-modal fusion design combines Sentinel-1 SAR and Sentinel-2 optical data to achieve excellent performance (accuracy: 89.52\%, F1-score: 90.85\%), leveraging the complementary information captured by SAR and optical modalities. SAR provides all-weather capability with sensitivity to surface roughness and moisture, while optical sensors offer rich spectral information for snow discrimination. The achieved performance demonstrates that intelligent fusion of these modalities enables accurate snow cover estimation across diverse conditions. The cross-attention fusion mechanism enables explicit modeling of bidirectional dependencies between SAR backscatter patterns and optical spectral signatures. The attention mechanism allows each modality to dynamically weight the relevance of information from the complementary modality, adapting to varying data quality and snow conditions. This design choice supports the model's robust performance across the full range of snow coverage scenarios tested (0.2\%-75.5\%), from near-snow-free conditions where optical information dominates to cloud-contaminated scenarios where SAR provides critical all-weather observations. The model's consistent performance across varying atmospheric conditions validates the fusion architecture's effectiveness at leveraging complementary sensor information. The quality-aware training framework further enhances fusion robustness by appropriately weighting samples based on data quality metadata, enabling the model to learn optimal sensor utilization patterns under diverse observation conditions.


\subsection{Quality-Aware Training Impact}

The quality-aware training framework contributes to the model's excellent overall performance (accuracy: 89.52\%, F1-score: 90.85\%) by enabling robust learning from heterogeneous satellite data with varying quality characteristics. Explicit incorporation of data quality information through multiplicative loss weighting enables the model to learn representations that generalize effectively across challenging atmospheric conditions and varying snow coverage scenarios (0.2\%-75.5\%). The integration of auxiliary metadata from multiple sources (Sentinel-2 AOT, WVP, SCL; GFSC QC, QCFLAGS) provided complementary quality indicators that collectively enhanced training efficiency. The adaptive weighting scheme $\text{weight} = (\text{confidence}^\alpha) \times (\text{quality\_mask}^\beta)$ effectively balanced sample diversity with quality emphasis, avoiding the pitfall of training exclusively on high-quality samples while still prioritizing reliable data.

The curriculum learning component, progressively relaxing confidence thresholds from $\tau_{init}=0.9$ to $\tau_{final}=0.3$, proved particularly valuable for handling the heterogeneous quality characteristics of satellite data. This progressive strategy enabled stable learning dynamics during early training while gradually exposing the model to more challenging scenarios, ultimately improving robustness to edge cases and degraded data conditions. The operational implications of quality-aware training are significant. Models trained with quality awareness demonstrate superior performance under real-world conditions where data quality varies spatially and temporally. The uncertainty estimates enable operational systems to automatically flag low-confidence predictions, reducing the risk of erroneous decisions in critical applications. Furthermore, the quality-aware framework facilitates efficient utilization of large but noisy satellite archives by extracting maximum value from available data rather than requiring extensive manual curation.

Limitations of the current quality-aware approach include reliance on pre-defined quality indicators that may not capture all sources of data degradation. Future work could explore learned quality assessment networks that automatically identify degraded data based on input characteristics, potentially improving robustness to unanticipated quality issues.


\subsection{Uncertainty Quantification and Reliability}

The uncertainty quantification framework provides operationally valuable insights into prediction reliability through spatially coherent uncertainty estimates. The framework successfully identifies regions where predictions are less reliable, with elevated uncertainty at snow-land boundaries and areas with complex topography, demonstrating physically realistic uncertainty estimation that aligns with known sources of ambiguity in snow detection. The operational value of uncertainty quantification extends beyond simple confidence flagging. Quantified uncertainties enable probabilistic decision-making frameworks where downstream applications can incorporate prediction uncertainty into risk assessments. For example, flood forecasting models could weight snow cover predictions by their uncertainty estimates, propagating uncertainty through hydrological models to generate probabilistic runoff predictions. Similarly, avalanche risk assessment systems could flag high-uncertainty regions for additional monitoring or ground validation.

Uncertainty analysis revealed that prediction confidence appropriately varies with data quality, surface complexity, and snow coverage characteristics. Elevated uncertainty in regions with limited training data representation (complex topography, rare surface types) or high atmospheric interference (cloud contamination, sensor noise) enables targeted quality control and adaptive processing strategies. This pattern enables targeted improvement strategies: increasing training data diversity for underrepresented scenarios and improving preprocessing algorithms to reduce uncertainty from atmospheric and sensor artifacts. The uncertainty framework's ability to appropriately increase estimates for out-of-distribution scenarios enhances operational reliability by flagging predictions that may require additional validation. Cross-domain validation confirmed effective separation of high-confidence from low-confidence predictions, reducing the risk of overconfident errors in atypical conditions.


\subsection{Limitations and Challenges}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Dataset Scope:} Our test set covers \deleted{4 geographically distinct tiles from northern Finland during winter 2025}\added{four geographically distinct W-band MGRS tiles from Northern Finland (December 2024--July 2025)}, demonstrating spatial generalization within a single season and geographic region through rigorous 70/15/15 train/val/test split with 30-day temporal separation. However, temporal generalization across multiple seasons and broader geographic regions (different latitudes, snow types, climatic zones) remains to be validated. The test set's 18,117 patches from unseen tiles provide strong evidence for spatial transferability, but year-round operational readiness requires multi-season evaluation. Future work should assess performance in mid-latitude regions with sporadic snow cover, forested environments with canopy interference, maritime vs. continental snow regimes, and high-altitude vs. lowland environments.

    \item \added{\textbf{Geographic Transferability:} Our study area-Northern Finland's boreal zone-features relatively flat to gently undulating terrain (typical slopes $<$5\textdegree), homogeneous boreal forest land cover, and predominantly dry continental snow. These conditions represent a favorable scenario for SAR-based fusion: gentle topography minimizes SAR geometric distortions (layover, foreshortening, radar shadow), and dry snow exhibits relatively stable C-band backscatter characteristics \cite{ulaby1986microwave, nagler2000improved}. Consequently, our reported performance metrics should be interpreted as achievable under near-optimal SAR acquisition geometry, and transferability to other geographic and climatic settings cannot be assumed without dedicated validation. \\ 
    In complex alpine terrain (e.g., European Alps, Himalayas, Andes), three interacting factors would present substantial challenges. \textit{First}, SAR geometric distortions scale nonlinearly with terrain slope relative to the radar look angle. At Sentinel-1's nominal incidence angles (29\textdegree--46\textdegree), foreslopes exceeding approximately 20\textdegree\ produce severe foreshortening where topographic relief is compressed in the range direction, while slopes approaching the complement of the incidence angle cause layover, where returns from multiple elevations collapse into the same range cell, rendering affected pixels physically uninterpretable \cite{tsai2019remote}. Radar shadow on steep back-slopes creates complete signal voids. Critically, these distortions concentrate in high-altitude accumulation zones where accurate snow monitoring is most needed for downstream water resource forecasting \cite{immerzeel2010large}. \textit{Second}, snow metamorphism varies substantially across climatic regimes, producing fundamentally different SAR-optical signatures. Maritime snowpacks (e.g., Scandinavian coast, Cascades) are warm, dense, and undergo frequent melt--freeze cycles that cause strong C-band absorption through liquid water content \cite{nagler2016advancements}; continental interior snowpacks (e.g., Rocky Mountains, Siberia) remain cold and dry with depth hoar and faceted grain growth exhibiting volume scattering; and transitional regimes (e.g., Himalayan basins) display complex diurnal melt--refreeze stratification varying with elevation \cite{snapir2019spatiotemporal}. Feature representations learned from northern Finland's predominantly dry boreal snow may therefore not generalize to regimes where SAR-snow interactions are governed by different physical mechanisms. \textit{Third}, the strong coupling between elevation, aspect, solar illumination geometry, and snow distribution in mountainous terrain creates spatial heterogeneity at scales far finer than observed in our study area, where snow distribution is governed primarily by vegetation canopy density and latitude rather than topographic position. \\  Several architectural properties of XFuse are relevant to these challenges, though their effectiveness in alpine settings remains unvalidated. The bidirectional cross-attention mechanism enables each modality to selectively attend to the other through learned query-key-value projections-in SAR-degraded regions, the attention weights could theoretically shift toward optical spectral features, analogous to the mechanism's designed behavior under cloud contamination but operating in the reverse direction. The quality-aware training framework, which learns continuous sample weighting from heterogeneous metadata, provides a natural extension point: terrain-derived quality indicators (local incidence angle, layover/shadow masks from DEM analysis) could be incorporated as additional metadata channels, enabling systematic down-weighting of geometrically distorted SAR observations. However, these remain architectural affordances rather than validated capabilities. \\  Robust geographic generalization would require: (1) radiometric terrain correction of Sentinel-1 data using high-resolution DEMs to mitigate slope-dependent radiometric biases; (2) incorporation of topographic variables (slope, aspect, curvature, sky view factor) as auxiliary model inputs through a dedicated terrain encoder path; (3) training on geographically diverse datasets spanning boreal, alpine, and maritime snow regimes to capture regime-dependent SAR-optical relationships; and (4) domain adaptation or transfer learning strategies to efficiently extend learned representations to undersampled snow climate zones. We consider multi-region validation across contrasting terrain types and snow climate regimes as the primary direction for extending this work toward globally applicable operational deployment.}

    \item \textbf{Comparison Scope:} We compare against three strong baseline architectures (Early Fusion, CNN-Only, Transformer-Only) trained on identical data, demonstrating 38.7\% MAE reduction and 5.5 percentage point accuracy improvement over the best baseline. We also reference published methods (HybridSN, CRformer, CVTNet, mSwinUNet) where metrics are available. However, direct comparison with operational snow products (MODIS MOD10A1F, VIIRS VNP10A1F, Sentinel-3 SLSTR) and traditional NDSI thresholding remains future work. Our focus on architectural innovations and systematic ablation studies demonstrates methodological contributions, but operational validation requires benchmarking against existing operational systems under identical evaluation protocols.

    \item \textbf{Computational Requirements:} Our hybrid architecture contains 47.1M parameters and requires 4.2GB GPU memory during inference, processing 16$\times$16 patches in $\sim$50ms on A100 GPU. Training completed in 34.6 hours on a single NVIDIA A30 GPU, demonstrating feasibility for standard research infrastructure. However, continental or global-scale deployment at full temporal resolution (daily Sentinel-1/2 acquisitions) would require distributed computing infrastructure or model compression techniques (pruning, quantization, knowledge distillation) to achieve operational throughput. Test-time augmentation increases inference time by 8$\times$ but provides significant reliability improvements, creating trade-offs between accuracy and computational efficiency that must be balanced based on application requirements.

    \item \textbf{Reference Data Limitations:} Training relies on Gap-Filled Fractional Snow Cover (GFSC) at 60m resolution as reference data, inheriting GFSC's own accuracy constraints. \added{Consequently, all quantitative metrics reported in this study (MAE, RMSE, accuracy, $R^2$) are computed at 60m resolution and represent consistency with the GFSC product rather than independently validated accuracy. The model's 10m prediction capability, achieved through guided disaggregation during inference, is validated qualitatively by visual alignment with high-resolution Sentinel-2 optical boundaries but lacks quantitative ground truth at sub-60m scales.} While our multi-resolution framework successfully leverages 10m input features through the $10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$ training strategy, fine-scale snow cover heterogeneity below 60m lacks direct supervision. The validation approach comparing predictions against GFSC may overestimate performance relative to independent ground truth. Future work should incorporate sparse high-resolution annotations from field surveys, airborne LiDAR, or UAV imagery to validate sub-60m prediction accuracy and provide complementary ground truth independent of GFSC.

    \item \textbf{Quality Metadata Dependency:} The quality-aware framework integrates auxiliary metadata (Sentinel-2 SCL, cloud masks, GFSC QC layers, atmospheric parameters) through multiplicative loss weighting. While this enhances training robustness, operational deployment requires consistent availability of quality information across all satellite acquisitions. Some older archive data or degraded processing scenarios may lack complete metadata. Robust fallback strategies (learned quality assessment, default weighting schemes) are essential for scenarios where quality information is incomplete or unreliable.

\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Broader Implications for Earth Observation}

\begin{enumerate}
    \item The methodological contributions of this work extend beyond snow cover monitoring to broader earth observation applications facing similar challenges. The multi-resolution training paradigm addresses a fundamental problem in remote sensing where high-resolution sensors lack corresponding high-resolution reference data. This approach is applicable to diverse applications including land cover classification, vegetation monitoring, and urban change detection, where training labels are often available only at coarser resolutions than input sensors.

    \item The quality-aware learning framework provides a systematic approach for handling the heterogeneous data quality inherent in operational satellite archives. As earth observation moves toward big data analytics leveraging decades of historical observations, methods for learning from imperfect data while quantifying uncertainty become increasingly critical. The demonstrated success of quality-aware training with auxiliary metadata integration offers a template for other applications to leverage available quality information rather than requiring extensive manual data curation.

    \item The cross-modal fusion architecture demonstrates the value of explicit modeling of inter-sensor relationships through attention mechanisms. As multi-sensor earth observation systems become increasingly common (Sentinel constellation, Landsat-Sentinel integration, commercial radar-optical combinations), sophisticated fusion approaches that capture complementary information will become essential for extracting maximum value from available observations.

    \item The uncertainty quantification framework addresses a critical gap in operational earth observation systems. While machine learning methods increasingly achieve competitive accuracy, their adoption in operational contexts is limited by lack of reliability indicators. The demonstrated calibration of uncertainty estimates (ECE=0.025) with spatially coherent patterns provides a pathway for responsible deployment of machine learning in critical earth observation applications.

    \item The hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture showcases the synergistic combination of inductive biases (CNNs for local feature extraction) with global modeling capabilities (Transformers for long-range dependencies). This architectural paradigm is increasingly relevant for earth observation applications where both fine-scale spatial detail and landscape-scale context are essential for accurate predictions. The demonstrated computational efficiency of the hybrid approach, operating on compact feature representations ($H/32 \times W/32$), addresses scalability concerns while maintaining modeling capacity.

    \item The broader implications extend to policy and decision-making contexts where earth observation data informs critical decisions. The quantified uncertainties enable risk-informed decision-making frameworks that explicitly account for observation uncertainty. The all-weather monitoring capability, combining SAR and optical observations, addresses operational requirements for continuous environmental monitoring independent of atmospheric conditions. These capabilities advance toward the vision of operational, reliable earth observation systems that support evidence-based environmental management and climate adaptation strategies.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Discussion}
